  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 1040 PM MST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nSlightly cooler temperatures are expected on Wednesday due to weak \ncold air advection aloft as eastern Colorado will be situated on the \neastern edge of the well-advertized upper level ridge. However, it \nwill still be warm, with highs in the low-to-mid 60s across the \nplains. Similar to the past few days, quiet weather will prevail \nwith mostly clear skies. \n\nOn Thursday and Friday, the axis of the upper level ridge will shift \neast, allowing slightly warmer temperatures to return to the \nregion. However, ensemble guidance has trended cooler these past \nfew runs, so we don't have as good of a chance to hit 70 F in the \nplains. This is reflected in DESI probabilities dropping to 20-25%\nchance of temperatures meeting or exceeding 70 F. Regardless, we \nwill stay dry across the region. \n\nGuidance continues to hone in on a system this weekend bringing \nmountain rain/snow showers and possibly precipitation to portions of \nthe plains. However, there are still many uncertainties with the \ntrack and evolution of this system that will impact how much \nmountain snow we will get and how widespread rain across the plains \ncould be. Recent runs of the GEFS and ECMWF ensemble have trended \ntowards a stronger shortwave trekking across Colorado, with some \nsolutions resolving a closed low near southeast Colorado. However, \nmodels diverge with the track of the low pressure after that, with \nsome tracks being too far south or east for our region to see the \nbulk of the expected precipitation. As of now, ensemble guidance is \nin agreement of light snow accumulations for the high country \n(mainly above 8000-8500 ft) Saturday through Sunday, and increasing \nchances for rain showers for our eastern plains on Sunday. It is \nstill too soon to tell if the rest of the plains, including the \nDenver metro would see measurable precipitation from this system. \n\nFall-like weather will likely return by Saturday or Sunday, with \ntemperatures nearing seasonal normals for the first time in a while. \nEnsemble guidance is in good agreement of cooler temperatures \nsticking around through the rest of the forecast period. There is a \ngood signal that active weather will return by mid-week next week \nwith multiple systems providing precipitation chances for the\nforecast area.",
